{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/958398-variable-spells-with-interfaces","id":958398,"title":"Variable spells with interfaces","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-26T13:55:39-07:00","id":958398,"title":"Variable spells with interfaces","content":"How do you go about making spells or enchanted items that have variable effects but use an interface that a non mage can use. Example: Say a group of mages enchant a time machine and wanted a sleep walker friend to be able to use it by turning some dials and such, a gun that has a dial that changes it from kill to stun, a mirror that changes your face by you choosing certain effects through an mind hologram display selection screen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11277-hyperspacewizard","name":"hyperspacewizard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11277_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T14:08:56-07:00","id":958406,"title":"","content":"Imbued Items.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T15:04:18-07:00","id":958439,"title":"","content":"A buttload of spells. Conditional Dispel Magic that goes &quot;ON&quot; and &quot;OFF&quot; based on position of the switch, and so on. In general, Fate and Prime are good for this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T16:05:15-07:00","id":958469,"title":"","content":"I don't think that's necessary. I mean the things they're describing are obviously Imbued Items that are Imbued with multiple spells and part of the activation trigger is having the dial set just so or whatever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T17:37:47-07:00","id":958518,"title":"","content":"When a mage uses a spell their able to see and interact with the spell say a mage uses velocity control the mage sets the potency is it possible for someone that isnt a mage to change the potency?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11277-hyperspacewizard","name":"hyperspacewizard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11277_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T17:41:39-07:00","id":958521,"title":"","content":"How would you create a constant effect that was variable? Like a magic prosthetic limb that works just like a real limb but has different settings for strength and dexterity?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11277-hyperspacewizard","name":"hyperspacewizard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11277_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T17:58:47-07:00","id":958529,"title":"","content":"No someone who isn't a Mage can't change the Potency, though that will happen as a result of a Sleeper witnessing it to cause Unraveling. Which decreases Potency. Even Mages can only decrease Potency on spells that are already cast.<br /> <br /> Prosthetic limbs are Imbued Items though you could do a Matter/Life conjunctional Patterning effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-26T19:28:35-07:00","id":958564,"title":"","content":"For a time machine how about Forces and Time effects that convert Forces, the movement of a lever, into Time distortions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11888-custos-tenebrarum","name":"Custos Tenebrarum","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T08:31:57-07:00","id":959264,"title":"","content":"I think any such item would have to have a number of spells with individual on-off triggers like WHW said. A spell that is truly variable, especially with a dial rather than a specific trigger for each spell, is the very definition of the Practice of Dynamics. A Time Machine that flexible would have to be a) a single but likely large object with individual spell functions tied specifically to gears, levers, and knobs (i would say it cant change potency, but CAN, say, decide what moment within the spell's range that you end up in), or b) a Dynamic spell-creation of an archmage at least, with a flexible, changing, reactive, almost self-aware spell inside that can adjust its own spell factors and such. Archmastery's first spell practice is one that is flexible compared to regular magic. With standard magic, each spell is exactly as it says it is. You can add Conditions, adjust factors, imbue it into an object, tickle it with Attainments, but ultijately it is just a single, standard spell. You get what you put into it--conditions and triggers, duration, potency. Each effect, trigger, condition etc has to be a separate spell. <br /> <br /> Dynamism points out a very big barrier in terms of magic. In 1e, archmastery was also defined by bringing folks back to life or travelling back in time. In 2e, those barriers no longer exist. But standard magic is really uniform. Your successes don't provide a gradient of effect--that's potency, which is set when you cast the spell. I would suspect in 2e, the real gateway drug of archmagic is reactive, dynamic spells--a spell that adapts to the user and situation, like the time dial on a time machine (complete with selective temporal barrier to keep out weather and environmental changes and such, but allow oxygen yet slow metabolism so you can breathe and not starve to death etc). A really kewl flexible and awe inspiring object usable by Sleepers should take a lot of time, dedication, planning, and clever use of spell control, that'd be at least 2-3 stories figuring out, designing, and implementing for a standard mage. Easy-made flexible time machines with one spell are Archmastery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T08:48:23-07:00","id":959277,"title":"","content":"Corridors of Time can be Imbued into an object the user can then target himself and send himself back anywhere on his own timeline that he wants to go provided he has the necessary Temporal Sympathy. The things that are set are the Reach (I assume) for how the spell will operate and the Duration for which he'll remain in his own past. You could probably set it up so it only takes its target back to when they're in 5th grade, but the way the spell is written you only require Temporal Sympathy to access an entire timeline. Imbued Items that allow a person to teleport work in a similar fashion. You don't really -need- an interface that changes which locations you go to except as it applies Sympathy to the item's spell.<br /> <br /> As for the other examples from the OP, it's obviously just two different spells imbued into one item with settings being used as part of the activation of the item. When the dial on the gun is set one way it meets part of the activation requirements for a death ray and when its set the other way it meets the activation requirements for a stun ray instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T08:57:24-07:00","id":959285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/958398-variable-spells-with-interfaces?p=959277#post959277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Corridors of Time can be Imbued into an object the user can then target himself and send himself back anywhere on his own timeline that he wants to go provided he has the necessary Temporal Sympathy. The things that are set are the Reach (I assume) for how the spell will operate and the Duration for which he'll remain in his own past. You could probably set it up so it only takes its target back to when they're in 5th grade, but the way the spell is written you only require Temporal Sympathy to access an entire timeline. Imbued Items that allow a person to teleport work in a similar fashion. You don't really -need- an interface that changes which locations you go to except as it applies Sympathy to the item's spell.<br /> <br /> As for the other examples from the OP, it's obviously just two different spells imbued into one item with settings being used as part of the activation of the item. When the dial on the gun is set one way it meets part of the activation requirements for a death ray and when its set the other way it meets the activation requirements for a stun ray instead.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. If you could get the effect done within a single set of spell factors and reach, such as Corridors of Time, I'd expect the device is pretty simple. The more complicated the &quot;interface&quot;, the more Spells will be needed, even the same spell with separate triggers per function. But at a certain point, the permutations will be too big of a headache and too big a spell control investment. At a certain point of interactivity, you should just shrug and say &quot;needs more Dynamism&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T08:19:59-07:00","id":959828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/958398-variable-spells-with-interfaces?p=959277#post959277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Corridors of Time can be Imbued into an object the user can then target himself and send himself back anywhere on his own timeline that he wants to go provided he has the necessary Temporal Sympathy. The things that are set are the Reach (I assume) for how the spell will operate and the Duration for which he'll remain in his own past. You could probably set it up so it only takes its target back to when they're in 5th grade, but the way the spell is written you only require Temporal Sympathy to access an entire timeline. Imbued Items that allow a person to teleport work in a similar fashion. You don't really -need- an interface that changes which locations you go to except as it applies Sympathy to the item's spell.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if I understand you correctly, but I don't think you can make an imbued item which brings the user to a time/place the user decides the moment he/she is activating it. While the Corridors of Time spell as well as any teleporting spell can theoretically bring you anywhere or anywhen, you do have to specify the time/place when you are actually casting the spell, not later on. If you want an item which can bring a subject to basically anywhen in time, you would need an infinite amount of corridors of time spells on the item.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11939,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11939-hardcorehannes","user":"HardcoreHannes","date":". 08-29-2016, 11:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11939-hardcorehannes","name":"HardcoreHannes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T10:04:39-07:00","id":959879,"title":"","content":"Remember that Patterning spells do not merely perform actions but actually grant functions to patterns. If you create a magical prosthetic limb, Potency simply determines the limits of the limb, its maximum Strength and Dexterity for example. However, the user of such limb can perform any number of actions that would be &quot;natural&quot; for it, it doesn't require a separate spell for each possible action or motion the limb can perform, unless the creator of the spell imposed such limitations when designing their Imago. If you turn into another animal with Life, you know how to use all of your limbs and have all the instincts and senses of that animal at your disposal, you don't need one spell per benefit. The same logic can be applied to any other number of spells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2535-arthexis","name":"arthexis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2535_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T10:57:01-07:00","id":959903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HardcoreHannes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/958398-variable-spells-with-interfaces?p=959828#post959828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't know if I understand you correctly, but I don't think you can make an imbued item which brings the user to a time/place the user decides the moment he/she is activating it. While the Corridors of Time spell as well as any teleporting spell can theoretically bring you anywhere or anywhen, you do have to specify the time/place when you are actually casting the spell, not later on. If you want an item which can bring a subject to basically anywhen in time, you would need an infinite amount of corridors of time spells on the item.</div> </div> </div> </div>When you activate an Imbued Item it casts the spell it's Imbued with. You have to pay the activation costs (At least 1 mana) and are restricted to the Factors that were set for the spell when the item was imbued and relinquished. Where in Time and Space isn't the target though, the person Teleport or Corridors of Time are cast on is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T06:40:09-07:00","id":960403,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/958398-variable-spells-with-interfaces?p=959903#post959903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> When you activate an Imbued Item it casts the spell it's Imbued with. You have to pay the activation costs (At least 1 mana) and are restricted to the Factors that were set for the spell when the item was imbued and relinquished. Where in Time and Space isn't the target though, the person Teleport or Corridors of Time are cast on is.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am aware of this, but when &quot;Corridors of Time&quot; is cast regularely the mage casting it has to decide where the subject goes at the moment he is forming the imago of the spell. He has to specify the time explicitely, he cannot cast a spell that is transporting the subject to where the subject wants to be in time.<br /> That said I really don't know how this works with imbued items. If e.g. a sleepwalker is activating an item which has the &quot;Corridors of Time&quot; spell imbued in it, he is automatically the subject of the spell, but who defines to what time he is going and how. I would normally say the time has to be specified by the caster who imbued the item. If this is not true, like you suggest, then how is the user of the item able to influence/change the imago of the spell, especially if he is not a mage. Or is the item somehow reading the mind of the user and includes this into the spell?<br /> If the first interpretation is true, that would also mean, that you cannot create a rod which throws fireballs at a specific target. You would need to aim the rod.<br /> I really don't know how this is supposed to work and both the imbued item merit as well as the attainment description are a bite vague on this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11939-hardcorehannes","name":"HardcoreHannes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T08:09:26-07:00","id":960444,"title":"","content":"They are vague, I just prefer the interpretation that allows for a greater level of versatility.<br /> <br /> Nitpick: The person activating an item imbued with CoT isn't automatically the target of the spell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}